\section{Details about control variables}
\label{sec:control_variables}

First, I discuss control variables I excluded.
I excluded controls variables that were causal intermediates in the marginal cost of life relationship.
Namely, I excluded control variables that represented the levels of treatments.
For, example, I excluded variables related to clean water, nutrition, and health care spending.
These all represent the level of specific treatments.
Because I am trying to esimate the relationship between life expectancy and the price of life, via these specific treatments, I needed to exclude these causal intermediates.

Through theory, but informed through automated searching through variables in the WDI, I determined a list of several control variables.

Unfortunately, the model with all control variables was unestimable.
By prioritizing variables that increase the $R^2$ of the regression the most, I was able to create an estimable model with all but a few of the variables.

I report the results with all estimable control variables in \autoref{sec:specification_appendix}.
With all these variables, the life expectantancy was significant at the 10\%, but not 5\%, level.
However, many of these control variables are highly correlated with modeled life expectancy, leading to inaccurate coefficient estimates.
I iteratively excluded control variables with a $GVIF^{\frac{1}{2 * df}}$ until the $GVIF^{\frac{1}{2 * df}}$ values for all control variables and life expectancy were below $\sqrt{10}$.
This a relatively conservative cut-off.
Unfortunately, this leaves open the possibility that the effect I observed was not due to modeled life expectancy, but one of the many variables collinear with modeled life expectancy.

Several control variables were insiginficant in the main regression.
I kept these variables for the main regression.
I excluded these insiginficant variables only the for the purposes of prediction model.

In \autoref{tab:control_variables_table}, I report the control variables I considered, along with their status.

