\section{Specification robustness tests}
\label{sec:specification_appendix}

There were a number of decisions I had to choose a model specification.

Ideally, I would use fixed effects by country and by year.
However, the confidence interval for the life expectancy coefficient was very wide and included zero.
This suggests there was not enough variation within countries in order to get an accurate estimate.

I also had to decide what to do about variation in price of life observations within a country-year.
Some country-years had as many as \rinline{max(filter_price_data(summarized_prices)$number_of_observations)} price of life observations, and others had none at all.

To correctly take into account this variation, I needed to use a hierarchical Bayesian model.
Instead of using modeled life expectancy in the second stage, I used observed life expectancy and modeled the residuals as correlated between the two stages.
This is typical of Bayesian instrumental variable models.
For each stage, I estimated random effects by country and year, as well as the correlation in these errors between the two stages.
Finally, I modeled random variation around a true mean for log price of life observations.
% TODO: look up formal names
I used standard normal priors for coefficients, exponential (1) priors for standard deviations, and lkj corr cholesky (2) priors for correlation matrices.

A simpler way to account for this variation is to average log price of life observations within a country year, and weight country-years by the number of observations.
I used this strategy for most models.
This is not a perfect fix, as the weighting should only apply to the second stage, not the first stage.
As a robustness test, I included an unweighted model.

For the main analysis, I decided to use a more traditional panel models.
All standard errors are robust to heteroskedasticity.
In addition, the pooled model standard errors are robust to within-country autocorrelation.

The results are in \autoref{tab:specification_robustness_table}.

